// @trap memory access out of bounds
// THE BOUNDS POLICY through a bulk op (§A4): the engine's check is the policy, and
// a copy whose destination range runs off the end of the memory traps.
//
// The copy below starts at 65534 and is 4 bytes wide, so it runs two bytes past the
// end of the one page this module has — the same arithmetic
// `load-past-page-end-traps.vl` uses for a load. `bulk-copy-and-fill.vl` performs
// the SAME copy successfully after a `__memory_grow__(1)`, which is what makes this
// a bounds trap rather than a page-boundary one: linear memory is flat, and a bulk
// op straddles the 64 KiB line freely when the bytes on both sides exist.
//
// Note also that the trap is ALL-OR-NOTHING in one respect worth knowing and one it
// is not: wasm requires the bounds check before any bytes move for the OOB portion,
// but a partially-in-bounds copy may leave the in-bounds prefix written. Nothing
// here depends on which, and no VL surface exposes the difference.
__store_i32__(1024, 67305985)
__memory_copy__(65534, 1024, 4)
print(1)
